✅ Same Abstract Method in Multiple Interfaces
If two interfaces declare the **same abstract method signature** (same name + same parameters + compatible return type),
and a class implements both, there is **no conflict** — implement the method **once**.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Example

```java
interface A {
    void display();
}

interface B {
    void display();
}

class MyClass implements A, B {
    @Override
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Display from MyClass");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyClass obj = new MyClass();
        obj.display(); // Output: Display from MyClass

        A a = obj;
        B b = obj;
        a.display(); // same method
        b.display(); // same method
    }
}
```

One implementation satisfies both interfaces.

────────────────────────────────────────
🧠 Why no conflict?
Abstract interface methods are only a **contract** (no body).
The class supplies a single body that fulfills both contracts.

────────────────────────────────────────
⚠️ When it DOES conflict

| Situation | Result |
| --------- | ------ |
| Same name + params + same/compatible return (abstract) | ✅ OK — implement once |
| Same name + params but **incompatible return types** (e.g. `int` vs `String`) | ❌ Compile error |
| Both interfaces have same method as **`default`** | ❌ Conflict — must override (see *Resolve default method conflict*) |

```java
interface A {
    int get();
}

interface B {
    String get(); // incompatible return type
}

// class X implements A, B {} // ❌ cannot implement both
```

Why?
- To satisfy `A`, class needs: `public int get()`
- To satisfy `B`, class needs: `public String get()`
- In Java you **cannot** have two methods with the same name + same parameters that differ only by return type
- So one class cannot fulfill both contracts → compile error

Contrast with the OK case (`void display()` in both): one method body works for both.

❓ “What if I override/implement both `get()` methods in `X`?”
Still ❌ impossible.

```java
class X implements A, B {
    public int get() { return 1; }
    public String get() { return "hi"; } // ❌ duplicate method — differs only by return type
}
```

Java sees both as the same method signature (`get()` with no params). Return type alone cannot distinguish them,
so the class itself won’t compile — implementing both doesn’t solve it.

Workaround: redesign (different method names, or one interface, or a wrapper type).

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Summary (Interview Answer)
"If two interfaces have the same abstract method, a class can implement both and provide that method once — one implementation covers both.
Conflict happens with default methods of the same signature, or when return types are incompatible."

🎤 Quick interview traps
1) Abstract + abstract (same signature) → no conflict.
2) Default + default (same signature) → conflict; must override.
3) Incompatible return types → cannot implement both interfaces.
4) Implementing once is enough; you don’t write `display()` twice.
